insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6914d92c-8813-5988-ae30-baed5619fd55', 'wg12-informative-writing-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Ancient Air in Ice Cores$q$, $q$(1) Deep beneath the surface of the Halberg Ice Sheet, snow that fell more than a hundred thousand years ago still holds tiny bubbles of ancient air. (2) Each winter, new snow buries the layer below it, and over centuries the weight above compresses the older layers into solid ice. (3) As the snow turns to ice, pockets of the surrounding atmosphere become sealed inside, preserving a sample of the air as it existed when each bubble closed.

(4) To retrieve these samples, scientists drill ice cores, long cylinders of ice extracted with a hollow drill. (5) In 2021, a team led by glaciologist Ama Owusu-Brandt drilled a core 2,400 meters long at the Teller Ridge research station. (6) In a freezer laboratory, researchers sliced the core into segments and crushed small pieces inside a vacuum chamber to release the trapped gases. (7) The station's cafeteria served hot soup every night, which the drillers appreciated after long shifts in the cold. (8) By measuring the carbon dioxide in each sample, the team built a record of the atmosphere stretching back about 120,000 years.

(9) Dating the ice is essential, because a gas measurement means little unless scientists know when the air was trapped. (10) In the upper part of the core, researchers can count annual layers much as one counts tree rings, since summer snow and winter snow differ in texture and chemistry. (11) Deeper down, the layers are squeezed too thin to count, so the team matched bands of ash from known volcanic eruptions to fix certain depths to specific dates.

(12) Ice cores are a thing that scientists use.$q$, 'draft')
