-- Passage wg12-informative-writing-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c50f4846-0f1f-5c8d-8ec2-48ea194da042', 'wg12-informative-writing-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cleaning a Darkened Painting$q$, $q$(1) Many paintings in museum collections look darker and more yellow today than they did when their artists finished them. (2) The main cause is varnish, a clear protective coating that painters and later owners brushed over finished works. (3) Over decades, many traditional varnishes oxidize, reacting with oxygen in the air and gradually turning amber or brown.

(4) Removing old varnish is one of the most delicate tasks a conservator performs. (5) A conservator first tests tiny areas, usually along the edges, with a series of solvents of increasing strength. (6) The goal is to find a solvent that dissolves the varnish without softening the paint beneath it. (7) Once the right solvent is chosen, the conservator rolls small cotton swabs across the surface, a few square centimeters at a time.

(8) In 2023, conservators at the Ellery Museum of Art spent fourteen months cleaning a large harbor scene by the painter Odile Marchetti. (9) Before treatment, the sky in the painting appeared a muddy green. (10) Beneath the yellowed varnish, the conservators discovered that Marchetti had painted the sky a clear, pale blue. (11) The museum gift shop now sells postcards of the painting.

(12) Conservators apply a new varnish made from a synthetic resin. (13) Unlike older coatings, these modern varnishes resist yellowing and can be removed easily with mild solvents if future conservators need to treat the painting again.$q$, 'draft')
